<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="Syntax">
        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`reactive(body)                 // no parameter — unkeyed and tracked
reactive(body, options)
reactive((args) => …)          // one parameter — keyed and untracked
reactive(async function* () {})
reactive(initial)              // a value the scope owns
reactive(initial, options)`} />

        <Section title="Parameters" level={3}>
            <ul>
                <li><code>body</code> — the function producing the value. Declaring
                <strong>no</strong> parameter makes an unkeyed, tracked value. Declaring
                <strong>one</strong> makes a keyed, untracked one, and that parameter is the cache
                key. Declaring it <code>async function*</code> makes it a stream, and so does
                handing back an async generator, or a promise of one, from any run. A body answering
                <code>undefined</code> lands <code>undefined</code>.</li>
                <li><code>initial</code> (optional) — a settled value or a load. Leaving it out is
                an empty value, <code>reactive&lt;T&gt;()</code>, where
                <code>reactive&lt;T&gt;(undefined)</code> has landed <code>undefined</code>.</li>
                <li><code>options</code> (optional) — the <code>ReactiveOptions</code> below.</li>
            </ul>
        </Section>

        <Section title="Return value" level={3}>
            <p>A <code>Reactive</code>, except from a keyed body, which hands back a
            <code>Keyed</code> — a callable handing back the <code>Reactive</code> for that key, so
            the probes and the triggers belong to the result and not to the factory.</p>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The factory">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Chunk, Shaped = Chunk[], Gate = Schema<Chunk[]>>(body: () => AsyncGenerator<Chunk> | Promise<AsyncGenerator<Chunk>>, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Chunk[], Kept<Shaped>, never>, \'schema\'> & { transform?: (value: Chunk[]) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Chunk[]> }) => Reactive<Kept<Shaped>, Chunk[], Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Chunk[]>, Chunk>'}</code></td><td>A stream. The value is the chunks so far, materialized on the read.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Chunk, Args, Shaped = Chunk[], Gate = Schema<Chunk[]>, ArgsGate = Schema<Args>>(body: (args: Args) => AsyncGenerator<Chunk> | Promise<AsyncGenerator<Chunk>>, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Chunk[], Kept<Shaped>, never, Args>, \'schema\' | \'args\'> & { transform?: (value: Chunk[]) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Chunk[]>; args?: ArgsGate & Schema<Args> }) => Keyed<Kept<Shaped>, Args, Chunk[], Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Chunk[]> | Gated<ArgsGate, Args>, Chunk>'}</code></td><td>A stream per args key.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Chunk, Args, Shaped = Chunk[], Gate = Schema<Chunk[]>, ArgsGate = Schema<Args | undefined>>(body: (args?: Args) => AsyncGenerator<Chunk> | Promise<AsyncGenerator<Chunk>>, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Chunk[], Kept<Shaped>, never, Args | undefined>, \'schema\' | \'args\'> & { transform?: (value: Chunk[]) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Chunk[]>; args?: ArgsGate & Schema<Args | undefined> }) => Keyed<Kept<Shaped>, Args | undefined, Chunk[], Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Chunk[]> | Gated<ArgsGate, Args | undefined>, Chunk>'}</code></td><td>A keyed stream whose parameter is optional: called with its record or without one.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Computed, Shaped = Awaited<Computed>, Gate = Schema<Awaited<Computed>>>(body: () => Computed, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Awaited<Computed>, Kept<Shaped>, never>, \'schema\'> & { transform?: (value: Awaited<Computed>) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Awaited<Computed>> }) => Reactive<Kept<Shaped>, Awaited<Computed>, AdoptedFailures<Computed> | Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Awaited<Computed>>, AdoptedProduced<Computed, Kept<Shaped>>>'}</code></td><td>Unkeyed. Recomputes when a value its body read changes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Computed, Args, Shaped = Awaited<Computed>, Gate = Schema<Awaited<Computed>>, ArgsGate = Schema<Args>>(body: (args: Args) => Computed, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Awaited<Computed>, Kept<Shaped>, never, Args>, \'schema\' | \'args\'> & { transform?: (value: Awaited<Computed>) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Awaited<Computed>>; args?: ArgsGate & Schema<Args> }) => Keyed<Kept<Shaped>, Args, Awaited<Computed>, AdoptedFailures<Computed> | Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Awaited<Computed>> | Gated<ArgsGate, Args>, AdoptedProduced<Computed, Kept<Shaped>>>'}</code></td><td>Keyed. One entry per args key, computed when that key is reached and held.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Computed, Args, Shaped = Awaited<Computed>, Gate = Schema<Awaited<Computed>>, ArgsGate = Schema<Args | undefined>>(body: (args?: Args) => Computed, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Awaited<Computed>, Kept<Shaped>, never, Args | undefined>, \'schema\' | \'args\'> & { transform?: (value: Awaited<Computed>) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Awaited<Computed>>; args?: ArgsGate & Schema<Args | undefined> }) => Keyed<Kept<Shaped>, Args | undefined, Awaited<Computed>, AdoptedFailures<Computed> | Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Awaited<Computed>> | Gated<ArgsGate, Args | undefined>, AdoptedProduced<Computed, Kept<Shaped>>>'}</code></td><td>A keyed value whose parameter is optional: called with its record or without one.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Accepted, Shaped = Accepted, Gate = Schema<Accepted>>(initial: Accepted | Promise<Accepted>, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Accepted, Kept<Shaped>, never>, \'schema\'> & { transform?: (value: Accepted) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Accepted> }) => Reactive<Kept<Shaped>, Accepted, Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Accepted>>'}</code></td><td>A value the scope owns, from a settled value or a load.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>{'<Accepted = undefined, Shaped = Accepted, Gate = Schema<Accepted>>(initial: undefined, options?: Omit<ReactiveOptions<Accepted, Kept<Shaped>, never>, \'schema\'> & { transform?: (value: Accepted) => Shaped; schema?: Gate & Schema<Accepted> }) => Reactive<Kept<Shaped>, Accepted, Refused<Shaped> | Gated<Gate, Accepted>>'}</code></td><td>A value opening at <code>undefined</code>. Loaded, and ready to read.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code></td><td><code>&lt;Accepted&gt;() =&gt; Reactive&lt;Kept&lt;Accepted&gt;, Accepted, Refused&lt;Accepted&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>A value nothing has written yet. Not ready until the first <code>set</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive.share</code></td><td><code>&lt;Key extends string, Value&gt;(key: Key, create: () =&gt; Reactive&lt;Value&gt;) =&gt; Key extends keyof Shared ? Reactive&lt;Shared[Key]&gt; : Reactive&lt;Value&gt;</code></td><td>Get-or-create in the component's scope by key, so two components reach one value without a prop between them.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Keyed</code></td><td><code>Keyed&lt;Stored, Args, Accepted = Stored, Failures = never, Produced = Stored&gt;</code></td><td>What a keyed body hands back: a callable carrying the probes and the triggers, the factory being what has an args space to narrow over. Each key reaches a <code>Reactive</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Args</code></td><td><code>Record&lt;string, JsonValue&gt; | undefined</code></td><td>The key. Serializable by contract, the key being the wire form.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Shared</code></td><td><code>{'interface Shared {}'}</code></td><td>The app's declaration-merged registry of shared keys.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The keyed face">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>k.busy</code></td><td><code>(pattern?: Partial&lt;Args&gt;) =&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Whether ANY matching entry has work in flight.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>k.ready</code></td><td><code>(pattern?: Partial&lt;Args&gt;) =&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Whether EVERY matching entry has a value to serve.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>k.invalidate</code></td><td><code>(pattern?: Partial&lt;Args&gt;) =&gt; void</code></td><td>Marks every matching entry stale.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>k.refresh</code></td><td><code>(pattern?: Partial&lt;Args&gt;) =&gt; void</code></td><td>Reloads every matching entry, serving what is held meanwhile.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`Reactive<
    Stored = undefined,
    Accepted = Stored,
    Failures = never,
    Produced = Stored,
>`} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="Type parameters">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>Stored</code></td><td><code>unknown</code></td><td>What is held, and what a read returns.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Accepted</code></td><td><code>unknown</code></td><td>What a factory and a write take, before the gates run. Identical to <code>Stored</code> unless a <code>transform</code> moves the two apart.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Failures</code></td><td>union of <code>Failed</code></td><td>The refusals this value's producer declared, narrowed by <code>s.isError</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Shaped</code></td><td><code>unknown</code></td><td>What a <code>transform</code> hands back: the value to store, or a refusal. With no <code>transform</code> it is <code>Accepted</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Produced</code></td><td><code>unknown</code></td><td>The unit the producer yielded: <code>Stored</code> for every producer but a stream, one chunk for a stream.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Options">
        <p>Every <code>Reactive</code> takes these, whichever overload made it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>schema</code></td><td><code>Schema&lt;Accepted&gt;</code></td><td>The gate on the way in. Refuses by throwing, and the throw is converted rather than escaping.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>transform</code></td><td><code>(value: Accepted) =&gt; Shaped</code></td><td>The shaping on the way to storage. Runs untracked, on the settled value, once per stored value.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>store</code></td><td><code>Store&lt;Stored&gt; | ((args: Args) =&gt; Store&lt;Stored&gt;)</code></td><td>Where the value lives when the process does not. Answers before the initial value, and <code>undefined</code> from <code>get</code> is a miss. Round-trips the latest production. On a value with a body, a trigger runs the body.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>identity</code></td><td><code>(next: Stored, previous: Stored) =&gt; unknown</code></td><td>What makes it the same value. A projection compared by <code>!==</code>, or a comparator answering directly.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>structural</code></td><td><code>(next: unknown, previous: unknown) =&gt; boolean</code></td><td>The by-value comparator an app declares on <code>identity</code>, whose default is the reference. Answers "not equal" wherever it cannot decide.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>by</code></td><td><code>(keyof Element &amp; string) | ((element: Element) =&gt; unknown)</code>, over the <code>Element</code> a <code>Stored</code> array holds</td><td>What tells one element of the value from another: the property it names, or what the function returns. The key of a <code>{'{#for}'}</code> over the value named alone, where the block writes none. Only the property-name form reaches a subscriber's copy over the wire. Separate from <code>identity</code>, which compares the whole value.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ttl</code></td><td><code>number</code></td><td>The life of a production, or of a keyed entry, in ms. Default infinity. At the deadline the production is released and the value reads as neither ready nor busy; nothing reloads until a read, which runs the producer. On a <code>Keyed</code> it is the life of the entry, and a <code>set</code> or a <code>patch</code> on the entry starts it again. On a value with no producer it is inert.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>args</code></td><td><code>Schema&lt;Args&gt;</code></td><td>The gate on a keyed value's args. It never decides which entry a key lands on, and the body gets what it returned. Refused at construction on a value with no parameter.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>global</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>One cache shared by every caller in the process, outliving every request.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>tags</code></td><td><code>string[] | ((args: Args) =&gt; string[])</code></td><td>What a <a href={url('/reference/selections')}>Selections</a> entry matches on, and the only thing that reaches across values.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>throttle</code></td><td><code>number</code></td><td>A change lands immediately, then at most once per window. What a window collapses depends on what moves the value — a write, a patch, a chunk, a reload. On a stream a read serves what the window last released. On a <code>Keyed</code> it holds back starting a newly reached entry, which reports busy meanwhile.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>debounce</code></td><td><code>number</code></td><td>A change waits until the changes stop. Declaring both is a build error.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <Section title="Supporting types" level={3}>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>Transformer</code></td><td><code>&lt;Accepted, Stored, Failures = never&gt;(value: Accepted) =&gt; Stored | Failures</code></td><td>What a <code>transform</code> is. Returning a <code>Failed</code> refuses the write.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Store</code></td><td><code>{'{ get: () => Stored | undefined | Promise<Stored | undefined>; set: (value: Stored, retention: { ttl: number }) => void | Promise<void> }'}</code></td><td>Both halves, and there is no way to spell either alone. A restore runs neither gate, so what it cannot read is a miss.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Section>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Reads">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; Stored</code></td><td>Reads the current value. Returns what it has and never awaits, and throws <code>NotLanded</code> where none has landed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.peek</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; Stored | undefined</code></td><td>Reads what is held without joining the flow. Answers <code>undefined</code> exactly where <code>s</code> throws.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>NotLanded</code></td><td><code>{'Error & { name: \'NotLanded\'; source: object }'}</code></td><td>What a read throws where nothing has landed. It carries the value it was thrown for, so a tracked body that meets one reruns when that value lands. A refusal is a different throw and a different catch.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.then</code></td><td><code>&lt;Result&gt;(onSettled?: (value: Stored) =&gt; Result | PromiseLike&lt;Result&gt;, onFailed?: (error: unknown) =&gt; Result | PromiseLike&lt;Result&gt;) =&gt; Promise&lt;Result&gt;</code></td><td>The settling, as a promise. Resolves when <code>busy()</code> goes false, and rejects with what a read would throw.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.catch</code></td><td><code>&lt;Result&gt;(onFailed: (error: unknown) =&gt; Result | PromiseLike&lt;Result&gt;) =&gt; Promise&lt;Stored | Result&gt;</code></td><td>The settling's refusal path, derived from <code>then</code>. Fires for a producer that failed with nothing landed, and for nothing else.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.finally</code></td><td><code>(onSettled: () =&gt; void) =&gt; Promise&lt;Stored&gt;</code></td><td>The settling's end, whichever way it went. What ends is the settling, not the value — a subscriber's copy of a channel settles at its first production and goes on.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>for await (… of s)</code></td><td><code>AsyncIterable&lt;Produced&gt;</code></td><td>The live cursor face of a read. On a stream, one turn per chunk. On any other value, the standing value once per turn — a burst collapses to the latest, and an in-place <code>patch</code> is a turn of its own.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s[Symbol.asyncIterator]</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; AsyncIterator&lt;Produced&gt;</code></td><td>The production in flight from its start, and then what follows it. A chunk is a piece of one value, so a stream's cursor sees every chunk; any other value's cursor sees where the value stands.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Writes">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.set</code></td><td><code>(value: Accepted | Promise&lt;Accepted&gt; | Reactive&lt;Accepted, unknown, Failures, unknown&gt;) =&gt; void | Failures</code></td><td>Writes here, gated by <code>identity</code>. Takes a settled value, a load, or a <code>Reactive</code> it adopts, and hands back the refusal a gate refused it with, or nothing. On a subscriber's copy it stands until the next production from the connection; on an adopting value it reaches the adopted one; on a stream it stands until the next chunk.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.patch</code></td><td><code>(mutate: (value: Stored) =&gt; void) =&gt; void</code></td><td>Mutates the value it serves in place, and runs neither <code>schema</code> nor <code>transform</code>. Where a <code>GET</code> serves the value, what it wrote is sent to every subscriber as the writes rather than as the value. On a stream it mutates the chunks themselves.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Probes">
        <p>A probe never throws and never starts work, and reading one subscribes to that probe
        alone.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.busy</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; boolean</code></td><td>Work is in flight for this value — a first load, a reload, or a stream that has not closed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.ready</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; boolean</code></td><td>There is a value to serve. A load in flight does not move it, so a reload reads as busy and ready at once.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.error</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; Failures | undefined</code></td><td>The standing refusal, cleared by the next accepted write.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.isError</code></td><td><code>{'<Name extends Failures[\'name\']>(error: unknown, name: Name) => error is Extract<Failures, { name: Name }>'}</code></td><td>Whether a caught failure is the one named. Matching narrows its data.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Triggers">
        <p>Both are inert on a value with no producer, so neither can discard what an app put there.
        On a value an rpc carries, both reach every subscriber's copy.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.invalidate</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; void</code></td><td>Drops the cache behind the value and not the value, marking what is held stale. Nothing loads and nobody is woken: it goes on being served, not busy, until the next read runs the producer.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.refresh</code></td><td><code>() =&gt; void</code></td><td>Stale while revalidate: what is held keeps being served until the new value lands. On a stream, until the new stream's first chunk.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Effects">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Signature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.watch</code></td><td><code>(effect: (value: Stored) =&gt; void | Disposer) =&gt; () =&gt; void</code></td><td>Runs an effect on value change, and hands back the way to stop it. See <a href={url('/reference/watch')}><code>watch</code></a>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Description">
        <p>Every factory hands this back, so nothing has to be wrapped in anything to have a face. A
        value given a load reports <code>busy()</code> and serves the settled value when it arrives;
        a value given a settled one has landed already and reports <code>ready()</code> from the
        moment it exists.</p>

        <p>Two types describe what goes in and what comes out. Without a <code>transform</code> they
        are one type, which is why <code>reactive&lt;number&gt;(0)</code> is the single type
        parameter it reads as.</p>

        <p>The overloads are ordered stream, unkeyed, keyed, value, and the order is forced. A body
        handing back an async generator also hands back something, so the general form would type a
        stream as holding its generator. And <code>() =&gt; C</code> is assignable to <code>(args:
        A) =&gt; C</code> and not the reverse, so a keyed-first reading would swallow the unkeyed
        form whole. Value last is why a function argument never reaches it — a function the app
        wants to hold goes inside a record, <code>{'reactive({ handler })'}</code>.</p>

        <p>A channel is a use of this one value rather than a kind of it: a <code>reactive</code>
        holding an array that a <code>GET</code> keeps sending to everyone connected. A message is
        appended with <code>room.patch((list) =&gt; list.push(message))</code>, the app trims in the
        same patch, and the latest message is <code>room.at(-1)</code>. Declaring <code>by</code> on
        it is what lets a <code>{'{#for}'}</code> over it move one row per message; without it the
        rows are positional, and trimming the front rebuilds every one.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="See also">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a></li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/watch')}><code>watch</code></a> ·
            <a href={url('/reference/selections')}>Selections</a></li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
